      <h2>4. Commutation of Pension: Recovery Duration and Restoration Demands</h2>
      <p>
        Under Central Civil Services (Commutation of Pension) Rules, a retiring government servant can commute up to a maximum of <strong>40% of their basic pension</strong> into a lump-sum cash advance at the time of retirement. 
      </p>
      <h3>The Current 15-Year Rule</h3>
      <p>
        Currently, the commuted portion is deducted from the retiree's monthly pension for a mandatory duration of <strong>15 years</strong>, after which the full basic pension is restored. For example, if an officer commutes 40% of a ₹50,000 basic pension (₹20,000 commuted), they receive ₹30,000 monthly for 15 years.
      </p>
      <h3>The 12-Year Restoration Demand</h3>
      <p>
        One of the most persistent, unified representations submitted by pensioner associations for the 8th Pay Commission is shortening the commutation recovery period from <strong>15 years to 12 years</strong>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The underlying mathematical argument is straightforward:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>When the 15-year rule was originally established decades ago, government borrowing rates and life expectancy tables were substantially different.</li>
        <li>Actuarial analysis demonstrates that the lump-sum principal advanced to the retiree, along with compounding interest at prevailing government rates, is completely recovered by the government within <strong>11 years and 6 months to 12 years</strong>.</li>
        <li>Continuing the deduction across years 13, 14, and 15 results in excess recovery from elderly citizens who face escalating medical burdens during their advanced golden years.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>5. Additional Quantum of Pension for Senior Pensioners (80+ Years)</h2>
      <p>
        The 6th Central Pay Commission introduced a crucial welfare measure: providing supplementary monthly financial support as retirees grow older and encounter severe geriatric medical costs. This structure was continued under the 7th CPC and will remain a foundational pillar under the 8th CPC.
      </p>
      <p>
        The additional pension is computed as an escalating percentage added to the basic pension:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Age 80 to under 85 years:</strong> 20% of revised basic pension</li>
        <li><strong>Age 85 to under 90 years:</strong> 30% of revised basic pension</li>
        <li><strong>Age 90 to under 95 years:</strong> 40% of revised basic pension</li>
        <li><strong>Age 95 to under 100 years:</strong> 50% of revised basic pension</li>
        <li><strong>Age 100 years or more (Centenarians):</strong> 100% of revised basic pension (Doubling the pension)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Proposal for Earlier Tiers:</strong> Parliamentary standing committees and pensioner federations have repeatedly urged the government to begin this additional quantum at age <strong>65, 70, or 75</strong> (e.g., granting a 5% hike at 65, 10% at 70, and 15% at 75), arguing that severe health issues typically manifest well before reaching 80. The 8th CPC provides the primary venue for evaluating this long-standing humanitarian reform.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Family Pension Provisions & Dearness Relief Mechanics</h2>
      <p>
        Family pension guarantees financial stability for surviving spouses and dependent family members following a pensioner's demise:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Normal Family Pension:</strong> Pegged at 30% of the deceased employee’s last drawn pay or corresponding revised basic pay. Under an expected fitment factor of 2.25x to 2.86x, the minimum family pension will escalate from the current ₹9,000 threshold to over ₹20,250 – ₹25,740 per month.</li>
        <li><strong>Enhanced Family Pension:</strong> Payable at 50% of last drawn pay for up to 10 years in case of service death, or up to 7 years (or until the deceased employee would have reached age 67) for superannuated retirees.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed Medical Allowance (FMA):</strong> For pensioners residing in non-CGHS covered regions, pensioner associations have lobbied for raising the current ₹1,000/month FMA to at least ₹3,000/month to keep pace with diagnostic and pharmacy expenditures.</li>
      </ul>

